{"text": "PHILIP MORRIS U.S. A. CONFIDENTIAL\n\nINTER-OFFICE CORRESPONDENCE\nRichmond, Virginia\n\nTo: Dr. R. H. Cox Date: February 11, 1993\nFrom: \u2014D.B. Spruill\nSubject: Alcohol Reduction: Cambridge and Bristol Aftercut\n\nCambridge (00-007) and Bristol (00-041) aftercuts have been reformulated as a result\nof reductions in organic solvents and total volume.\n\nCambridge\n\nSD-4 alcohol was reduced from the current level of 11.626 gallons per 10,000 Ib.\ntotal blend to 3 gallons. Casing 70 was reduced from 6.275 gallons to 4.7 gallons. Water\nwas used as a substitute, and the total volume of the finished A/C was reduced from 25\ngallons/10,000 1b. total blend to 20 gallons. Replicate I-liter lab samples of the\nreformulated A/C have been made and observed for 14 days at 24\u00b0C. Samples have remained\ntransparent with no \"fallout.\" Normally, a color change will occur after several days from\nblue-green to brown. All preblends remain the same per 10,000 Ib. as before.\n\nBristol\n\n\u2018SD-4 alcohol has been reduced from the current level of 15 gallons per 10,000 Ib.\ntotal blend to 5 gallons. Casing 70 has been reduced from 9.87 gallons to 8.37 gallons.\n\u2018Water was used as a substitute and the total volume of the finished A/C was reduced from 31\ngallons/10,000 Ib. total blend to 20 gallons. Replicate I-liter lab samples of the\nreformulated A/C have been made and observed for 14 days at 24\u00b0C, Samples have remained\ntransparent with no \u201cfallout.\u201d Color should be dark green-brown initially to dark brown\nafter a few days. All preblends remain the same per 10,000 Ib. of total blend.\n\nPertinent analytical data (G.C. profiles, % wt/wt of solvents/humectants and tracer\nflavor components) are provided for reference.\n\nD.B. Spruill\nDBS:Igk\n\nAttachment\n\nSohOTTESOZ",
